  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P(∅)=\\{∅\\}$\n\nII. $P(a)=\\{∅,\\{a\\}\\}$\n\nIII. $P(\\{∅,\\{∅\\}\\})=\\{∅,\\{∅,\\{∅\\}\\}\\}$",
  "worked_solution": "The power set of $T,$ denoted $P(T),$ is the set that contains every possible subset of $T,$ including the empty set $∅.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the power set of the set $∅$ is $\\{∅\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that $a$ is not a set. So, the notation $P(a)$ does not make sense.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that we are given the set $\\{∅,\\{∅\\}\\}.$ So, its power set must be $\\{∅,\\{∅\\},\\{\\{∅\\}\\},\\{∅,\\{∅\\}\\}\\}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
